                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "obligation",
                    "title": "must, mustn't, have to, don't need to, can",
                    "explanation": "«Must» expresa obligación, sobre todo en normas o cuando la impone quien habla. «Have to» también expresa obligación (a menudo externa) y en pasado se convierte en «had to»:\n- «The new flatmate must be clean and quiet.»\n\nAtención a la gran diferencia:\n- «mustn't» = está prohibido: «They mustn't smoke in the house.»\n- «don't have to» / «don't need to» = no es necesario: «You don't need to put an advert online.»\n\n«Can» / «can't» expresan permiso:\n- «Can they have a pet? — No, you can't have pets.»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Modal",
                        "Significado",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "must",
                          "obligación (norma)",
                          "The new flatmate must be clean and quiet."
                        ],
                        [
                          "have to / has to",
                          "obligación",
                          "Everyone has to pay a deposit."
                        ],
                        [
                          "mustn't",
                          "prohibición",
                          "They mustn't smoke in the house."
                        ],
                        [
                          "don't have to / don't need to",
                          "no es necesario",
                          "You don't need to put an advert online."
                        ],
                        [
                          "can",
                          "permiso",
                          "You can ask him."
                        ],
                        [
                          "can't",
                          "no está permitido",
                          "You can't have pets."
                        ],
                        [
                          "had to",
                          "obligación en pasado",
                          "You had to pay one too, Sam!"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "The new flatmate must be clean and quiet.",
                      "They mustn't smoke in the house.",
                      "Everyone has to pay a deposit.",
                      "You don't need to put an advert online."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "obligation",
                    "title": "ought to",
                    "explanation": "«Ought to» + verbo significa casi lo mismo que «should»: lo que es buena idea o lo correcto:\n- «You ought to tell Mrs Green about the noise.»\n- «We ought to clean the kitchen before Ollie arrives.»\n\nEs un poco más formal y menos frecuente que «should», sobre todo en preguntas, donde casi siempre se usa «should».\n\nLa negativa es «ought not to» («oughtn't to»), aunque en la conversación se prefiere «shouldn't»:\n- «You oughtn't to smoke in the house.»\n\nNo cambia con «he», «she» o «it» (nunca «oughts») y siempre lleva «to», a diferencia de «should» y «must» (nunca «ought go»):\n- «You ought to go.»\n\nPara la obligación fuerte o las normas se usa «must» / «have to»; para un consejo, «should» / «ought to».",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo",
                        "Fuerza"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "must / have to",
                          "Everyone has to pay a deposit.",
                          "obligación"
                        ],
                        [
                          "should / ought to",
                          "You ought to put an advert online.",
                          "consejo, lo correcto"
                        ],
                        [
                          "shouldn't / oughtn't to",
                          "You shouldn't play music after eleven.",
                          "no es buena idea"
                        ],
                        [
                          "don't need to",
                          "You don't need to pay for the internet.",
                          "no es necesario"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "We ought to clean the kitchen before Ollie comes.",
                      "Sam ought to turn the music down after eleven.",
                      "You ought to ask Mrs Green before you get a cat.",
                      "The new flatmate ought to meet the neighbours."
                    ]
                  }
                },
